\mt1 Philemon
\c 1
\s1 Greetings from Paul and Timothy
\r (\ref Philippians 1:1–2|PHP 1:1-2\ref*; \ref Colossians 1:1–2|COL 1:1-2\ref*)
\p \v 1 Paul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, and Timothy \add our\add* brother,
\p To Philemon our beloved fellow worker, \v 2 to Apphia our sister, to Archippus our fellow soldier, and to the church \add that meets\add* at your\f + \fr 1:2 \fqa You \ft and \fqa Your \ft are singular throughout this letter, except in verses 3, 22, and 25.\f* house:
\p \v 3 Grace and peace to you from God our Father and \add the\add* Lord Jesus Christ.
\s1 Philemon’s Faith and Love
\p \v 4 I always thank my God, remembering you in my prayers, \v 5 because I hear about your faith in the Lord Jesus and your love for all the saints. \v 6 \add I pray\add* that your partnership in the faith may become effective as you fully acknowledge every good \add thing\add* that \add is\add* ours in Christ. \v 7 I take great joy and encouragement in your love, because you, brother, have refreshed the hearts of the saints.
\s1 Paul’s Appeal for Onesimus
\p \v 8 So \add although\add* in Christ I am bold enough to order you \add to do\add* what \add is\add* proper, \v 9 I prefer to appeal on the basis of love. \add For\add* I, Paul, am now aged, and a prisoner of Christ Jesus as well.
\p \v 10 I appeal to you for my child Onesimus,\f + \fr 1:10 \fqa Onesimus \ft means \fqa useful \ft (see verse 11) or \fqa beneficial \ft (see verse 20).\f* whose father I became \add while I was\add* in chains. \v 11 Formerly \add he was\add* useless to you, but now \add he has become\add* useful both to you and to me. \v 12 I am sending back to you him who is my very heart.
\p \v 13 I would have liked to keep \add him\add* with me, so that on your behalf he could minister to me in \add my\add* chains for the gospel. \v 14 But I did \add not\add* want to do \add anything\add* without your consent, so that your goodness will not be out of compulsion, but by your own free will. \v 15 For perhaps this is why he was separated \add from you\add* for a while, so that you might have him back for good — \v 16 no longer as a slave, but better than a slave, \add as\add* a beloved brother. \add He is\add* especially \add beloved \add* to me, but even more so to you, both in person and in \add the\add* Lord.
\p \v 17 So if you consider me a partner, receive him as \add you would receive\add* me. \v 18 But if he has wronged you in any way or owes \add you anything\add*, charge it to my account. \v 19 I, Paul, write \add this\add* with my own hand. I will repay \add it\add*— not to mention that you owe me your very self.
\p \v 20 Yes, brother, let me have some benefit\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Greek \fqa onaimēn\ft , a play on the name \fqa Onesimus\f* from you in \add the\add* Lord. Refresh my heart in Christ.
\p \v 21 Confident of your obedience, I write to you, knowing that you will do even more than I ask.
\p \v 22 In the meantime, prepare a guest room for me, because I hope that through your prayers I will be restored to you.
\s1 Additional Greetings
\p \v 23 Epaphras, my fellow prisoner in Christ Jesus, sends you greetings, \v 24 \add as do\add* Mark, Aristarchus, Demas, \add and\add* Luke, my fellow workers.
\p \v 25 The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ \add be\add* with your spirit.\f + \fr 1:25 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa Amen.\f*
